# Fixtures are identified by the hash of their bytes: the manifests beside
# them record a sha256, the loaders check it, and the goldens assert it. So
# git must not normalise anything in this tree. A normalised copy is no
# longer the file that was fetched, and its hash is a hash of our own
# rewriting rather than of the agency's answer.
#
# This is written broadly on purpose. The rule was first added for the
# WATSTORE peak files alone, and the next fixture that arrived with CRLF
# line endings, a Reclamation area-capacity table, hit exactly the same
# failure: green locally, red in CI, because a working tree that never
# round tripped through a checkout still held the original bytes.
verification/fixtures/** -text
